# The Content-Security-Policy, and why every directive is exactly this wide.
#
# This app opens files it did not write. A .mbrd is a renamed ZIP anybody can
# hand-edit, a dropped .docx or .svg is somebody else's document, and an .md is
# somebody else's markup. The XSS thinking for all of that is already done and
# done in the code: ui/markdown.ts and ui/documents.ts build every node through
# createElement and createTextNode so there is no escaping to get right, and the
# one path that puts foreign markup on the page - SVG - is parsed detached and
# walked against an allow-list. This file is that same reasoning written
# somewhere the *browser* enforces it, so that the day somebody reaches for
# innerHTML with a string out of a file, they get a console error rather than a
# shipped bug.
#
# It is a second lock on a door that is already locked. That is the point: the
# first lock is a convention, and a convention is only as good as the next
# person to read the header comment.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this file, and why it is not in wrangler.jsonc
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The same reasoning wrangler.jsonc gives for expressing the not-found rule as a
# literal 404 page: _headers is the Netlify, Pages and Workers spelling of one
# rule, so the deploy does not depend on which host is under it. Cloudflare
# Workers static assets reads this file out of the assets directory and does not
# serve it.
#
# It is also the reason a violation cannot be seen locally: tools/serve.py sends
# no headers, so the dev server runs with no policy at all and the deploy is the
# first place anything is enforced. tests/csp.test.js is the substitute - it
# holds the hashes and the hosts below in step with the code they came from, so
# a change that would break the deploy fails in `npm test` instead.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# script-src 'self' + four hashes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# No 'unsafe-inline', which is the whole value of the directive: with hashes
# present a browser ignores 'unsafe-inline' for scripts anyway, and an injected
# <script> - inline or not - has nowhere to come from.
#
# The four hashes are the four inline scripts this app ships, and none of them
# can become a file:
#
#   - index.html's pre-paint appearance restore. Running before first paint is
#     its entire purpose: it reads the saved whimsy level, palette and token
#     overrides out of localStorage and puts them on <html> so an installed PWA
#     never flashes the default pigment on launch. A module cannot do that - it
#     is fetched, and a fetch is a frame. tests/appearance.test.js holds its
#     token grammar in step with ui/look.ts; this file holds its bytes in step
#     with the deploy.
#   - index.html's boot-cover rescue. Its second job is to uncover the page when
#     main.js never parses - a module 404 behind a stale service worker, a
#     syntax error, a browser below the floor. A failsafe for "the modules did
#     not load" cannot itself be a module.
#   - index.html's application/ld+json block. The HTML spec returns out of
#     "prepare the script element" for a data block before the CSP check ever
#     runs, so by the letter of it this hash is unnecessary. It is here because
#     implementations have not always agreed, the cost is one token, and a
#     search-engine description that silently stops being parsed is exactly the
#     kind of breakage nobody notices for a year.
#   - patch.html's two application/ld+json blocks, the breadcrumb and the
#     article. Exactly the reasoning one bullet up, at exactly the same cost of
#     one token each. The changelog is a page whose whole job is to be found and
#     read rather than used, so its structured data going quietly unparsed is
#     the failure it can least afford - and unlike index.html, that data is the
#     only thing telling a crawler this page belongs to the app rather than
#     being a second, unrelated root.
#
#     They are also the only thing on that page a policy has to know about. It
#     loads three stylesheets and runs no script whatsoever - no inline <style>,
#     no style="..." attribute, nothing to execute - which is deliberate and is
#     stated at the top of assets/css/patch.css.
#   - lab.html's inline module. The pigment lab is one page with its script in
#     it; it ships in web/ so it is covered by /* like everything else.
#
# 404.html is a byte copy of index.html (see save.bat), so it needs no hashes of
# its own - it carries the same two, and if it ever stops doing so
# tests/notfound.test.js fails first.
#
# What tightening further would cost: moving any of the three index.html blocks
# into a file, which is the only way to drop a hash, breaks the thing each one
# exists to do. Hashes are the tight answer here, not the loose one.
#
# No 'unsafe-eval' and no 'wasm-unsafe-eval': nothing in the app calls eval or
# new Function, and the one piece of WebAssembly it can run - the vendored
# ffmpeg core - runs in a worker with its own policy. See the second rule below.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# style-src 'self' + two hashes - no 'unsafe-inline'
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This was the one directive wider than it should be, and it is not any more.
# The three things that forced 'unsafe-inline' were:
#
#   - ui/feed.ts's waveform, which wrote `<i style="height:N%">` per bar into
#     innerHTML. A hash cannot cover a value computed at runtime, so this was
#     the blocker: it now builds the bars and assigns .style, which is CSSOM.
#   - the style="..." attributes in lab.html's generated preview card, same fix.
#   - the last static one, a font-size on a span in lab.html, now a class.
#
# With the attributes gone the two inline <style> blocks can be hashed, which
# is what the two sha256 values below are - one for the block in index.html, one
# for lab.html. Hashes and 'unsafe-inline' cannot be mixed anyway: a browser
# ignores the keyword the moment a hash is present, which is exactly the
# property being relied on here.
#
# The cost of this is a real maintenance edge and is worth stating plainly.
# Editing either <style> block by one character changes its hash, and the
# deploy will then refuse to apply that block - the page renders unstyled rather
# than failing loudly. tests/csp.test.js recomputes both and fails first, which
# is the only reason this is a reasonable trade rather than a trap.
#
# It does NOT cover ui/look.ts and ui/appearance.ts writing custom properties
# onto :root, or the pre-paint script doing the same. Those go through
# style.setProperty(), which is CSSOM and outside CSP by design.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# img-src / media-src / font-src - 'self' plus the two local schemes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There is no remote host in any of the three, and that is not an accident of
# this file - it is a property of the app that this file pins down. A board
# renders the same with the network off: link cards refuse to fetch a favicon or
# a preview, and nothing anywhere resolves a URL out of a document it opened.
# These three directives are what make that a guarantee instead of a habit, and
# they are the ones that would have caught a "renderer follows a URL out of a
# .mbrd" bug outright, at the browser, whatever the code did.
#
#   img-src 'self' blob: data:
#     'self'  - assets/icons.svg, assets/img/*, the sticker sprite.
#     blob:   - every picture on the board. Assets live in IndexedDB and reach
#               an <img> as an object URL (storage/assets.ts, canvas/display.ts,
#               canvas/renderers.ts, ui/documents.ts).
#     data:   - two uses, both ours: the board thumbnails in the library, which
#               ui/snapshot.ts makes with canvas.toDataURL, and the small SVG
#               glyphs written into custom properties in items.css and
#               sidebar.css. Dropping data: blanks the library grid and the pin
#               and chevron marks.
#
#   media-src 'self' blob:
#     Audio and video are the same object URLs. No data: - nothing produces a
#     data URL for a clip and a 40 MB one would be absurd.
#
#   font-src 'self' blob:
#     'self' is the bundled woff2 set. blob: is load-bearing and easy to miss:
#     a font dropped on the board is registered with
#     `new FontFace(family, 'url("blob:...")')` in ui/fonts.ts, and a FontFace
#     built from a url() IS checked against font-src. Without blob: every
#     imported font fails to load and the look silently falls back.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# connect-src 'self' blob: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Exactly three things fetch:
#
#   'self'    - ui/snapshot.ts reads assets/stickers.svg as text, and sw.js
#               fetches same-origin requests it is caching. The service worker
#               steps aside for anything cross-origin, so it needs nothing else.
#   blob:     - ui/pigments.ts fetches an asset's own object URL to get bytes
#               for createImageBitmap, deliberately rather than pointing an
#               <img> at it. A fetch of a blob: URL is a connect, not an image.
#   jsdelivr  - optimize/media.ts probes the vendored ffmpeg core with a
#               one-byte ranged GET before offering to download it. This is the
#               only outbound request the app makes on its own, and it is the
#               single reason a remote host appears anywhere in this file.
#
# Pinned to the host, not the path: CSP has no useful path matching for this and
# pretending otherwise would read as a promise the directive cannot keep.
#
# Dropping jsdelivr does not break anything hard - mediaAvailable() answers
# false and video optimisation and HEVC posters are quietly not offered, the
# same graceful skip the module already takes when the CDN is down. It is here
# because "quietly not offered" is a bad way to find out about a header.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# frame-src - the two embed hosts, and nothing else
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# canvas/embed.ts is the only place in mbrd that will ever talk to a third
# party, and it builds its src from a fixed template with a validated id in the
# hole - eleven characters of base64url for YouTube, twenty-two of base62 for
# Spotify. These two hosts are those two templates:
#
#   https://www.youtube-nocookie.com  - the embed src, chosen as the most
#                                       private host YouTube offers.
#   https://open.spotify.com          - the embed src for every Spotify kind.
#
# No wildcard, and deliberately not play.spotify.com or youtu.be even though
# spotifyRef and youTubeId accept those as *input*: what a user may paste and
# what a frame may load are different lists, and only the second one belongs
# here. tests/csp.test.js checks both directions against embed.ts, so adding a
# provider without adding it here fails in the suite.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The deliberate 'none's
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   object-src 'none'      There is no <object>, <embed> or <applet> in this
#                          app and there is never going to be. A plugin
#                          document is the oldest way to get script onto a page
#                          out of a file, which is precisely the thing this app
#                          does all day.
#
#   form-action 'none'     There is not a single <form> in the tree - nothing is
#                          ever posted anywhere, because there is no server.
#                          'none' says that; 'self' would leave a submission
#                          target for an injected form to aim at.
#
#   frame-ancestors 'none' Nobody may frame the app. There is no read-only or
#                          embeddable mode, so a page that frames it is either
#                          a mistake or a clickjack over a canvas where every
#                          click moves somebody's work. If the marketing site
#                          ever wants a live demo in an iframe, this is the line
#                          to widen - to 'self' https://valjdakosta.com, not to
#                          '*'. This also supersedes X-Frame-Options, which is
#                          why there is no X-Frame-Options here.
#
#   base-uri 'self'        NOT 'none', and this is the one place where the tight
#                          answer is wrong: index.html carries <base href="/">
#                          and it is load-bearing, because the same document is
#                          served as the 404 at arbitrary paths and every
#                          relative asset URL in it has to keep resolving from
#                          the root. 'none' would kill that tag and the app
#                          would fetch its modules from whatever directory the
#                          bad URL happened to name. 'self' keeps the tag legal
#                          and stops an injected <base> from pointing the whole
#                          document's relative URLs at another origin.
#
#   default-src 'self'     The backstop for every directive not named above -
#                          child-src, prefetch-src, and whatever CSP adds next.
#                          Same-origin, which is what this app is.
#
# worker-src and manifest-src are 'self' rather than left to default-src for one
# reason: they are the two that are easy to break by accident and hard to notice
# (a worker that will not spawn is a feature that quietly stops being offered; a
# manifest that will not load is an app that quietly stops installing), and a
# directive somebody can grep for is worth four characters.
#
# Not here on purpose: 'unsafe-eval' and 'wasm-unsafe-eval' (nothing needs
# either), upgrade-insecure-requests (there is not one http:// subresource in
# the tree, so it would upgrade nothing), require-trusted-types-for (about
# twenty innerHTML writes with constant icon markup would have to move first,
# and that is a refactor, not a header), and report-to (there is no server to
# report to, which is the whole architecture).

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-TXFmC+WkJ7pej6GG4eilTgWXAPfRfnxeO2p8iCMvh/k=' 'sha256-DkygY3dxoCj3zPATesYHPgON9mIeqlu6UbH2c+3mStw=' 'sha256-eJzJ8523EitoBKAS5A1tCSnraL7SAmf2gesGT63wGSk=' 'sha256-7MOXuzYEVnwtGN5LaHmnA8It5SPyIoYzkvOEItbnkGg=' 'sha256-Q8ZzrQgcVPkApKR0qNf6mroKHffRQsjhyVVZIRu0QtU=' 'sha256-8ZGO4EWxaESz6+qYFa7Px/8iyW9D2UaZh/+P4/mg99Q='; style-src 'self' 'sha256-wkVFcbpQLLLncGzQDSypPX4g+PgpgKuYUfkJ3D7xrA8=' 'sha256-9MwbduHoYcvGbMyIKsXKkXLfI0H0ks+ah6sfj4TrmeA='; img-src 'self' blob: data:; media-src 'self' blob:; font-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self' blob: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net; worker-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://open.spotify.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# The one exemption, and the only interesting thing about the whole file.
#
# A dedicated worker does not inherit the document's policy. It gets its own,
# from the headers on its own script response - and since the rule above is /*,
# that response would carry the app's policy. The app's policy forbids exactly
# the two things this worker exists to do: importScripts() a 32 MB Emscripten
# core from jsdelivr, and compile the wasm inside it (which needs
# 'wasm-unsafe-eval' in script-src). Under the rule above the encoder never
# starts, and the way that shows is video optimisation and HEVC posters silently
# not being offered - see the header of optimize/media.ts, where "the CDN is
# down" and "the core would not load" are already the same outcome.
#
# So the header is removed for that one file and the worker runs as it does
# today, with no policy. It is 60 lines that speak four messages and hold no
# state but the core, it touches no DOM because it has none, and it can reach
# nothing of this page.
#
# Removed rather than replaced, and that is not laziness: matching rules in a
# _headers file are *cumulative*, and two Content-Security-Policy values are
# joined with a comma - which a browser reads as two policies to satisfy at
# once, not as one that replaces the other. A looser policy on this path would
# therefore be intersected with the strict one above and change nothing.
#
# If a host ever ignores the removal, the worker keeps the strict policy and the
# encoder is not offered. That is a degradation the module already handles by
# design, which is what makes this safe to get wrong.
/assets/js/optimize/media-worker.js
  ! Content-Security-Policy
